@page "/learn/callback"
@model SmartOnFhirDemo.Pages.Learn.CallbackModel
@{
    ViewData["Title"] = "Back from the EHR";
}

<partial name="_Progress" model="new[] { Model.Step.Number }" />

<partial name="_Step" model="Model.Step" />

@*
    The code travels back in the form rather than being stashed on the server between the two
    requests. The browser is already holding it — address bar and history — the moment the
    EHR redirects here, so a server-side copy would be a second place it lives, not a safer
    one. What the page *shows* is still only enough of it to recognise.
*@
<form method="post" class="next">
    <input type="hidden" asp-for="Code" />
    <input type="hidden" asp-for="State" />
    <button type="submit">Exchange the code &rarr;</button>
</form>

<aside>
    Worth knowing before you linger: the SMART specification expects an authorization code
    to expire <em>“within around one minute”</em>. The SMART App Launcher is more generous
    at five, which is what makes this pause practical at all. Against a production EHR the
    same pause would often fail — which is itself the reason the plain launch does not take it.
</aside>
